  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nOvernight Monday, a closed heights center positioned over the \nNorthern Rockies negatively tilts as it lifts into south-central\nCanada. Widespread showers and storms (80-100% coverage) are \nexpected along the cold front moving in from the northwest, but \nlackluster mid-level lapse rates will put a hamper on the \noverall instability (750-1500 J/kg of SBCAPE). This is \nforecasted to, once again, limit the overall severe potential.\nHowever, there is a brief window where a mid-level jet boosts \nthe 0-6-km shear along the front to 45+ knots north of I-80. The\nSPC has maintained a Marginal Risk, Level 1 out of 5, for \nsevere storms across the far western counties, with the primary\nhazard being damaging winds. By Tuesday, storm coverage and \ninstability decreases as upper- level dynamical support weakens.\nThe cold front is forecast to stall out over northern Missouri,\nsimilar to today's set up, keeping storm chances around through\nthe end of the week.\n\nThe second half of the week is going to remain stormy. A ridge \nover the Southeast and a slow-moving shortwave trough over the \nwestern CONUS is forecast to pull multiple, weak subtropical \ntroughs northeastward into the Midwest. With the stationary \nfront from Tuesday's system acting as the source of lift, \nscattered showers and storms will likely develop daily. With \nsaturated grounds expected after the rounds of rain today \nthrough Tuesday, the flood threat will become more elevated with\neach subsequent round, and WPC has highlighted the area with a\nSlight Risk (level 2 of 4) for excessive rainfall through much\nof the week.\n\nIn terms of rainfall totals, latest LREF probability for 2\"+ are\n60-80% northwest of the Quad Cities and 20-40% to the southeast.\nThere is uncertainty though in the axis (or axes) of heaviest \nrainfall with the LREF 10th-90th percentile spread showing a 2-3\ninch difference areawide. Therefore, while the probabilities \nare low (10-20% across the NW outlook area, much lower \nelsewhere) for 5+ inches of rainfall, the ensemble spread is \nsuggesting that latest model guidance may be undercutting the \nhigh-end totals.",
